<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Okjeo</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 56 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A coastal people of northeastern Korea, known from Chinese records for their family ossuaries and tribute of fish and salt before Goguryeo absorbed them.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Okjeo lay along the coast of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-hamgyong-province">South Hamgyong</a> province. After Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gojoseon">Gojoseon</a> in 108 BCE the area was placed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuantu-commandery">Xuantu</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, and Chinese writers distinguished an Eastern Okjeo on the coast from a Northern Okjeo further inland.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chinese historians describe villages led by hereditary chiefs with no overall king, farming fertile coastal land and paying tribute of fish, salt and seafood. Two customs stood out: girls were betrothed young and raised in their future husband's household, and the bones of a whole family were placed in a single large wooden coffin.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> made Okjeo a tributary under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goguryeo">Taejo</a>, traditionally in 56 CE, and later absorbed it fully, drawing on its marine products. Okjeo's people merged into Goguryeo society.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/okjeo.jpg" alt="Okjeo" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Okjeo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 56 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>North Korea</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Okcho, Woju, East Okjeo</dd></dl></aside></div>

